GoldLink's change to melonDS (UC12): the steady overworld's attached objects (the player, their
shadow, a mount, a follower: GPU3D marks their polygons, patch 0022) in a picture of their own,
put together with the world's by one function, as the DS would draw the two in one picture.

UC11 drew them on the world's picture at their own targets and the window slid them apart from
it by a fraction of a pixel; everything the DS works out between them and the world (depth,
outlines, edges blended with what's behind, shadows) was then baked into one picture, and taking
it apart left gaps, ghosts and outlines (the user's play tests, 2026-11-14).

- GPU3D_Soft: two pictures, each with its own colour, depth, attributes, shadow stencil and
  translucent pixels: the world's (Main*) and the attached objects' (Att*, nothing in it). Drawn
  line by line together. With attached objects drawn, neither picture's final pass marks edges or
  blends them (anti-aliasing): Composite does, on the two put together.
- Each attached pixel keeps what it does to what's behind it, k * behind + b (AttK, AttB): opaque,
  nothing, or its shadow, an anti-aliased edge in between, with the DS's own blending as if the
  world were behind it (with nothing behind it in this picture the DS writes it unblended). Two of
  its own translucent pixels keep the stronger, and over the world's own translucent pixels it
  takes the darker of the two (WorldOpaque: the world before them): overlapping shadows read as
  one, the user's choice. Its translucent pixels keep their own depth (AttTransDepth). No fog in
  the attached picture (its empty pixels would fog).
- The world's translucent polygons the game draws after the attached objects' go over them (the
  DS blends in drawing order): per pixel the world before them and what they do (LateEarly,
  LateK, LateB) with the nearest one's depth.
- Composite(pictures, the world's pixel, the attached picture's, the offset between them): the
  nearer on top, its edge marked where a neighbour (in its own picture's pixels; whichever
  picture is on top there) has another polygon ID and is farther, its edge blended with what's
  behind it there (anti-aliasing). Combo: the two put together at the DS's pixels (the attached
  picture is drawn on the world's): what the 2D engine shows (GetLine, GetWideLine). The
  pictures as shown (GetShownPictures, GetShownAttachedLine) for the window, which calls
  Composite at the screen's resolution with the attached picture where it truly is.

Applied by build.rs to a copy of the submodule (which stays at upstream).

